\section{Phase-Strategy Crossover Details}
\label{app:crossover-details}

The crossover is present at all four tested sparsity levels ($s \geq 0.15$): at each level, importance-based eviction achieves lower II in the high-sparsity phase but higher II in the low-sparsity phase relative to random eviction. The magnitude of the low-phase reversal grows from $3.9{\times}$ at $s{=}0.15$ to $5.4{\times}$ at $s{=}0.20$, before moderating to $3.6{\times}$ at $s{=}0.30$ as random eviction severity also rises. % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030, bf16_ds_rand_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json; ratios: 0.398/0.103=3.87, 1.496/0.276=5.42, 7.196/1.363=5.28, 14.639/4.041=3.62

The crossover emerges from the phase-strategy interaction: in the high-sparsity phase---where attention is already focused---importance-based eviction preserves genuinely critical positions, yielding lower compound severity than random eviction. In the low-sparsity phase, however, head pruning redistributes attention mass across surviving heads, altering the importance score landscape in a way that couples the two sparsity mechanisms. The result is that targeted eviction inadvertently removes positions that would have been retained under the pre-pruning importance ranking---producing \emph{higher} compound severity than the explicitly random baseline.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig3_crossover.pdf}
\caption{Strategy-dependent crossover at $s{=}0.25$. Grouped bar chart of median II by eviction strategy and reasoning phase. Importance-based eviction outperforms random in the high-sparsity phase but produces $5.3{\times}$ higher II in the low-sparsity phase, reversing the strategy ranking.} % exp_id: bf16_importance_s025, bf16_ds_rand_s025; 7.196/1.363=5.28
\label{fig:crossover}
\end{figure}


\section{Cross-Model Replication Details}
\label{app:cross-model-details}

Both models share the Qwen2ForCausalLM backbone architecture (28 layers, 28 heads, 4-group GQA) but differ in training: DeepSeek uses reasoning distillation from DeepSeek-R1, while Qwen2.5 uses standard instruction tuning without explicit reasoning delimiters. This controlled comparison isolates the effect of training methodology on the compound sparsity interaction.

Despite lacking explicit \texttt{<think>} delimiters, Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct exhibits sufficient variation in attention sparsity across its generation trajectory to permit phase segmentation via the same quantile-based method (\S\ref{sec:phase-seg}). Four of the 50 problems are excluded due to degenerate phase assignments, yielding $n{=}46$ valid problems for Qwen (vs.\ $n{=}50$ for DeepSeek).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model replication: median compound severity difference ($\Delta = \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{low}} - \widetilde{\mathrm{II}}_{\textit{high}}$) under importance-based eviction.}
\label{tab:cross-model}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
Model & \footnotesize $s{=}0.15$ & \footnotesize $s{=}0.20$ & \footnotesize $s{=}0.25$ & \footnotesize $s{=}0.30$ \\
\midrule
DeepSeek-R1-7B & 0.39 & 1.48 & 7.16 & 14.59 \\ % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json
Qwen2.5-7B     & 0.04 & 0.23 & 1.27 & 2.94 \\ % exp_id: bf16_qw_imp_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json; 0.234979 rounds to 0.23 at 2dp
\bottomrule
\multicolumn{5}{@{}p{\columnwidth}@{}}{\footnotesize Both models share the Qwen2 architecture but differ in training (distillation vs.\ instruction tuning). DeepSeek-R1-7B = DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B; Qwen2.5-7B = Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct.}
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Per-problem concordance.}
The per-problem concordance across models is not significant (mean $\rho = -0.11$, $p = 0.926$), indicating that while the aggregate gradient direction replicates, the specific problems driving severity differ between models---consistent with model-specific attention patterns rather than a universal per-problem vulnerability ranking. The Qwen effect is marginal at $s{=}0.15$ ($d_z{=}0.28$, bootstrap CI includes zero), becoming reliable at $s \geq 0.20$ ($d_z \geq 0.55$); this onset lag relative to DeepSeek is consistent with the lower overall compound severity in instruction-tuned models lacking explicit reasoning structure. % exp_id: cross_model_analysis.json (spearman_rho=1.0, gradient_permutation_p=0.043, per_problem_concordance_p=0.926, mean_rho=-0.107); cohens_d_bf16_qwen.json

\paragraph{Random eviction control.}
As a control, random eviction on Qwen yields no systematic phase gradient at three of four sparsity levels (3/4 FAIL), mirroring DeepSeek's 4/4 FAIL pattern. At $s{=}0.30$, Qwen random produces a borderline PASS (permutation $p = 0.012$) but with negligible effect size ($d_z = -0.04$, CI spans zero), indicating no meaningful phase modulation. % exp_id: bf16_qw_rand_s015..s030; source: experiment_summary_table.json

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig5_crossmodel.pdf}
\caption{Cross-model comparison of the median severity difference under importance-based eviction. Both DeepSeek (solid) and Qwen (dashed) exhibit monotonically increasing $\Delta$ with sparsity; Qwen's magnitude is approximately one-fifth of DeepSeek's.} % exp_id: bf16_importance_s015..s030, bf16_qw_imp_s015..s030
\label{fig:cross-model}
\end{figure}


\section{Statistical Validation Details}
\label{app:stat-validation-details}

\paragraph{Permutation test.}
For each problem $i$ and sparsity level $s$, we compute the compound severity difference $\Delta_i^{(\text{strat})} = \mathrm{II}_{\textit{low},i} - \mathrm{II}_{\textit{high},i}$ under each eviction strategy. The test statistic is the median of the paired difference $\widetilde{\Delta} = \text{median}_i\!\bigl(\Delta_i^{(\text{imp})} - \Delta_i^{(\text{rand})}\bigr)$, measuring whether importance-based eviction produces systematically larger phase-dependent severity than random eviction. We permute strategy labels $10{,}000$ times to construct the null distribution and apply Bonferroni correction for 4 sparsity-level comparisons.

Table~\ref{tab:permutation} reports the results. All four sparsity levels reject $H_0$ (no strategy difference) after Bonferroni correction ($p_{\text{Bonf}} < 0.005$), with observed median differences increasing monotonically from $0.37$ at $s{=}0.15$ to $9.85$ at $s{=}0.30$. % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest; source: permutation_test_results.json

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Paired permutation test for compound severity: importance vs.\ random eviction on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B ($n{=}49$, $10{,}000$ permutations, Bonferroni-corrected).}
\label{tab:permutation}
\begin{tabular}{@{}rrcl@{}}
\toprule
$s$ & Observed $\widetilde{\Delta}$ & 95\% CI & $p_{\text{Bonf}}$ \\
\midrule
0.15 & 0.37 & $[0.19,\; 0.62]$ & 0.0032 \\ % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest_s015; source: permutation_test_results.json s015.permutation_test_difference
0.20 & 0.97 & $[0.69,\; 1.49]$ & ${<}\,0.001$ \\ % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest_s020; source: permutation_test_results.json s020.permutation_test_difference
0.25 & 3.34 & $[1.66,\; 6.64]$ & ${<}\,0.001$ \\ % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest_s025; source: permutation_test_results.json s025.permutation_test_difference
0.30 & 9.85 & $[6.70,\; 16.29]$ & ${<}\,0.001$ \\ % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest_s030; source: permutation_test_results.json s030.permutation_test_difference
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The monotonic growth of both the point estimate and the CI lower bound ($0.19 \to 0.69 \to 1.66 \to 6.70$) confirms that compound severity amplification under importance-based eviction strengthens with sparsity---not merely in central tendency but also in worst-case lower bounds. Figure~\ref{fig:permtest-forest} presents these results as a forest plot. % exp_id: deepseek_bf16_permtest; source: permutation_test_results.json

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig4_bootstrap.pdf}
\caption{Forest plot of permutation test results for the difference statistic. Horizontal bars show 95\% permutation CIs; diamonds mark observed median differences $\widetilde{\Delta}$. All CIs lie entirely above zero (vertical dashed line), confirming significant strategy-dependent severity at every sparsity level.}
\label{fig:permtest-forest}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Effect sizes.}
Table~\ref{tab:cohens-d} reports paired $d_z$ (bootstrap $B{=}10{,}000$) measuring the standardized phase-dependent II difference under importance-based eviction.

